  "issues": [
    "MISSING WORK: MO-GRPO (arXiv:2509.22047, Sep 2025) directly addresses reward hacking in multi-objective GRPO by normalizing advantage functions per objective to prevent variance-based dominance. This is a direct competitor to AGRC for gaming-resistant multi-objective GRPO training and must be cited and differentiated. Key difference: MO-GRPO is objective-agnostic (normalizes by variance), while AGRC uses gaming-specific signals (GRS). Add MO-GRPO as an additional baseline (C11) or discuss why variance normalization is insufficient for gaming resistance.",
    "MISSING WORK: 'When Reward Hacking Rebounds' (arXiv:2604.01476, April 2026) proposes Advantage Modification — integrating shortcut concept scores into GRPO advantage computation to penalize hacking rollouts. This is mechanistically similar to AGRC's gaming-aware training signal and should be discussed. Also missing: 'Mitigating Reward Hacking in RLHF via Advantage Sign Robustness' (arXiv:2604.02986, April 2026), which down-weights non-robust completions. Both represent alternative gaming-aware GRPO modifications.",
    "SCOPE OVERLOAD: The proposal now has 10 training configurations, 4 GRS metric variants, 3 cross-method comparisons, 2 model scales, a theoretical contamination model, a DRW head-to-head, a critic scaling analysis, and a broader applicability discussion — all in one paper. At ICLR, reviewers expect a clean narrative with 2-3 sharp contributions, not a 10-dimensional empirical matrix. Consider trimming: drop the GRIFT comparison (heavyweight, requires gradient computation per sample), reduce to 7 core configs, and defer the MI variant of GRS to supplementary material.",
